<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<worksheet>
  <title>Reading Quiz 06: Impulse and Momentum</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>
      <alert>Contents:</alert> This quiz covers sections 4.5 and 4.6
    </p>
    <p>
      <alert>Instructions:</alert> Answer all the questions to the best of your ability and upload your work to the 
      <q>Quiz 6</q> assignment in Canvas.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <page>
    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
            Is there a physical reason to define the muzzle <q>velocity</q> <m>v_0</m> in example 4.8 as a 
            relative speed? If not, then why not just give the speed of the ball relative to the ground?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Look at the paragraph at example 4.9 that says <q>Conservation of momentum is accurate here (<ellipsis/>)</q>.
          This is still true when we include gravity. How does the result of example 4.10 help you justify that?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          	Go back to example 4.8: what can you say about the momentum <m>P_y</m> in this example? 
            Try to give your answer as the impulse of a specific force
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <statement>
            <p>
                If we added friction between the launcher and the ground, would we still be able to use conservation of 
                <m>P_x</m> to find the velocity of the launcher right after the collision? Why or why not?
            </p>
        </statement>
    </exercise>

  </page>
</worksheet>